Most AI headlines are about GPUs, the chips that run the models. Agents add a second kind of work: calling tools, running code, moving data and keeping track of long tasks. Nvidia says that work needs a different processor, and it has built one. This post explains Vera using two Nvidia pages and marks which statements are claims.

*Disclosure: this post was drafted by Claude, an AI assistant made by Anthropic, and checked by a separate AI reviewer against the cited Nvidia pages for figures, dates and wording. This is not a full human review. Anthropic is named on one of the pages as a recipient of early Vera units, so treat any link between the companies with that in mind. Stackbrook did not test the product and has no affiliate relationship with any company mentioned at the time of writing. The header image is AI-generated.*

## What Vera is

Vera is a central processing unit, or CPU, designed by Nvidia for data centres. Nvidia's 16 March 2026 launch release describes it as built for agentic AI and says it is "in full production". The 27 August 2026 update of Nvidia's blog says it is the company's first CPU built for agents and is shipping now. The blog lists the jobs it targets: orchestration, tool-calling, reinforcement-learning workloads, data analytics, agent sandboxing and long-context state management.

The idea is simple. A GPU does the heavy model maths, but something has to feed it data, run the code an agent writes and manage many sessions at once. A CPU handles that, and Nvidia says Vera feeds GPUs more efficiently.

## The specs Nvidia publishes

Both pages agree on 88 custom Nvidia-designed cores, which the launch release calls Olympus. The launch release adds that each core can run two tasks using what Nvidia calls Spatial Multithreading, and that memory is LPDDR5X with up to 1.2 TB/s of bandwidth. The blog repeats the 1.2 TB/s figure.

When paired with GPUs in the Vera Rubin NVL72 platform, the release says the link between CPU and GPU is NVLink-C2C with 1.8 TB/s of coherent bandwidth, which Nvidia puts at 7x PCIe Gen 6. The blog describes Vera as the host processor there, sharing a unified memory design with two Rubin GPUs.

A Vera rack, per the release, holds 256 liquid-cooled CPUs and can sustain more than 22,500 concurrent CPU environments, each running independently. Configurations come in single and dual socket. Neither page gives memory capacity, absolute power draw, clock speed, process node or price. The release does claim the memory delivers twice the bandwidth at half the power of general-purpose CPUs, with no named baseline.

